  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 309 PM CDT Thu Oct 29 2026\n\nSunday through Thursday:\n\nSunday has trended drier as high pressure that is expected to \nbe over the eastern half of the US looks to hold fast and push \nrain chances southeast into the Ohio River Valley. High \ntemperatures will rebound into the mid 60s for highs Sunday as mixing\nwill tap into 925mb temps around 13 C aloft.\n\nA fairly active weather pattern is then expected to set up for\nnext week, as the GFS and ECMWF show an occluding low pressure\nsystem over the northern plains interacting with a closed low\naloft from central Canada and broad 500mb troughing of the\nsubtropical Jet over the central US. The occlusion of the low\nwill exposes us to WAA driven rain Monday night into Tuesday \nand then potentially a slow moving surface trough and more rain\nWednesday into Thursday. Given a lot of moving atmospheric \nparts coming together to form next week's weather system, a lot \nof uncertainty exists with regard to the timing and exact \nevolution of the system. For now better certainty exists for \nrain Monday night into Tuesday, with a far murkier picture \nbeyond. Stay tuned through the weekend for increases in forecast\nconfidence. \n\nGiven the occluding system will wrap a lot of warm air into it,\nnext week does look potentially mild with highs in the 60s Mon\nthru Wednesday, with temperatures then finally feeling more\nfall-lake late next week.\n\nCMiller",
